#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068395 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7229f1d0-4ba7-50ff-b5be-5697c886a175" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Compound Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "0901e241-519b-50ee-ab5f-50b2f631e948" :question/math-academy-id "e-13932" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $0 \\leq 3x+6 < 15$." :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 0 &\\leq 3x+6 < 15 \\\\[5pt] 0 - 6 &\\leq 3x + 6 -6 < 15-6 \\\\[5pt] -6 &\\leq 3x < 9 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{-6}{3} &\\leq \\dfrac{3x}{3} < \\dfrac{9}{3} \\\\[5pt] -2 &\\leq x < 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq x < 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068396 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5930113e-6971-59c9-810a-97fd25bc4f89" :knowledge-point/title "Compound Inequalities That Require Flipping the Inequality Sign"}] :question/id #uuid "8208b5ba-8c54-5c05-a9f4-04aa30f8892f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13933" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-1 < -2x + 1 \\leq 7$." :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & < - 2x + 1 \\le 7 \\\\ -1 - 1 & < - 2x + 1 - 1 \\le 7 - 1 \\\\ -2 & < - 2x \\le 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by $-2$. However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & < - 2x \\le 6 \\\\ \\frac{-2}{-2} & >\\frac{-2x}{-2}\\ge\\frac{6}{-2} \\\\ 1 & > x \\ge - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as\n\n$$\n1 > x \\geq - 3\n$$\n\nbut this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x < 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068397 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3ff37f0-6e45-5da4-ac87-1b93c8df3e80" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Solutions of Compound Inequalities on Number Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "7f671658-055a-599d-8f1f-416570e604b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-13935" :question/problem "Represent the solution to the inequality $-3 < 1-2x < 11$ using a number line." :question/worked-solution "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & < 1 - 2x < 11 \\\\ -3 - 1 & < 1 - 2x - 1 < 11 - 1 \\\\ -4 & < - 2x < 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-2$. Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & < - 2x < 10 \\\\ \\frac{-4}{-2} & > \\frac{-2x}{-2} > \\frac{10}{-2} \\\\ 2 & > x > - 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $2 > x > -5$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n$$\n-5 < x < 2\n$$\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/388/Source/Images/692aff95e86f691ddb4f9957282f51c5.png)"}]}
